## Mandatory scope addendum — GitHub Actions / CI deliverables

This is part of #252 and must be implemented before `RC1_ACCEPTED`. It is **not** a follow-up issue.

At the start of #252, `.github/workflows/` contains only `sonar_check.yml`. RC1 stabilization must therefore create/reconcile the complete CI execution layer below.

### 1. PR/Push quality workflow
Create:

```text
.github/workflows/python-quality-gate.yml
```

Requirements:
- trigger on pull requests targeting `main`;
- trigger on pushes to `main`;
- use the repository-supported primary Python version;
- install Tiny Swarm World and required quality/test dependencies reproducibly;
- execute the repository-owned canonical quality command, preferably `python tools/quality_gate.py quality`;
- fail whenever the canonical gate fails;
- keep workflow permissions least-privilege (`contents: read` unless more is proven necessary);
- do not duplicate Ruff/mypy/test configuration in YAML;
- preserve useful redacted failure evidence.

### 2. Python compatibility matrix using Conda
Create:

```text
.github/workflows/python-compatibility.yml
```

This implements the GitHub pattern **“Python Package using Anaconda — Create and test a Python package on multiple Python versions using Anaconda for package management.”**

Requirements:
- derive the supported Python-version matrix from authoritative project metadata/documentation; do not guess versions;
- use `conda-incubator/setup-miniconda` or the current maintained equivalent;
- create one isolated Conda environment per matrix entry;
- install Tiny Swarm World from the checked-out source;
- call repository-owned canonical unit/compatibility commands;
- set `strategy.fail-fast: false` so every supported Python version produces a result;
- expose every Python version as an independently visible GitHub job;
- never silently turn an unsupported/failing version green;
- treat compatibility failures as RC1 release evidence.

Conda is the isolated environment/package-management layer for this workflow. Existing project metadata such as `pyproject.toml` remains authoritative unless an authoritative `environment.yml` already exists.

### 3. Nightly Classic live / RC1 workflow
Create:

```text
.github/workflows/nightly-classic-live.yml
```

Requirements:
- scheduled nightly trigger;
- `workflow_dispatch` for controlled manual execution;
- invoke canonical Classic live runners/tests rather than reproducing installer logic in YAML;
- execute only on a runner capable of Classic requirements: Incus/LXC, Docker inside managed nodes, Swarm, networking and required privileges;
- if GitHub-hosted runners cannot provide that safely, define/document a self-hosted runner strategy;
- an unevaluated live path must never count as green RC1 evidence;
- cover the automatable lifecycle subset: Fresh Install -> Post-install acceptance -> Reconcile -> Post-reconcile acceptance -> Update -> Post-update acceptance -> Recovery;
- use bounded timeouts;
- use concurrency protection so destructive/live jobs cannot overlap on the same environment;
- upload only redacted RC1 evidence/artifacts.

### 4. Reconcile existing Sonar workflow
Inspect:

```text
.github/workflows/sonar_check.yml
```

Classify as one of:
- `REUSE_AS_IS`
- `EXTEND`
- `MERGE`
- `REPLACE_WITH_REASON`

Rules:
- no duplicate Sonar analysis;
- no contradictory second quality gate;
- preserve required Sonar/SonarCloud behavior;
- document which stable job/status-check names are intended for branch protection;
- never expose Sonar/GitHub/Infisical credentials in logs or uploaded evidence.

### 5. Required target structure
Unless Three Amigos explicitly consolidate it with documented rationale:

```text
.github/workflows/
  sonar_check.yml
  python-quality-gate.yml
  python-compatibility.yml
  nightly-classic-live.yml
```

Responsibilities must remain independently observable:

```text
PR/Push Quality
Python Compatibility Matrix
Sonar / Code Quality
Classic Nightly Live / RC1
```

### 6. Workflow validation
Every created/modified workflow must prove:
- valid YAML;
- all referenced actions exist and are intentionally version-pinned according to repository security policy;
- every invoked repository command exists and has the expected CLI contract;
- PR trigger works;
- push trigger works;
- Python matrix expands to every supported version;
- a deliberately failing test/quality command makes the workflow fail;
- failed matrix jobs cannot be masked by successful jobs;
- nightly/manual live jobs cannot overlap destructively;
- timeouts and cleanup are bounded;
- artifacts/evidence contain no secrets;
- status-check names are stable enough for branch protection.

### 7. CI evidence required for final RC1 candidate
Record:
- workflow file and commit SHA;
- workflow run ID/URL where applicable;
- trigger type;
- Python/matrix version;
- canonical command executed;
- result and duration;
- evidence/artifact location;
- secret-redaction confirmation;
- intended required status-check name.

Mandatory final CI gates:

```text
[PASS] PR/Push python-quality-gate
[PASS] Python compatibility matrix for every supported Python version
[PASS] reconciled Sonar/code-quality workflow
[PASS] nightly/manual Classic live execution on a valid Classic-capable runner
[PASS] workflow failure semantics proven
[PASS] redacted CI evidence
```

### 8. Additional implementation slices for #252
Add these to the execution order:
1. Inventory current `.github/workflows/` together with current tools/tests.
2. Three-Amigos CI layout and supported-Python matrix decision.
3. Reconcile `sonar_check.yml` and define stable status checks.
4. Create `python-quality-gate.yml`.
5. Create Conda-based `python-compatibility.yml`.
6. Create `nightly-classic-live.yml` with valid runner strategy/concurrency/timeouts/evidence.
7. Execute and verify all mandatory GitHub Actions gates before final RC1 decision.

### 9. Additional acceptance criteria
#252 cannot be `RC1_ACCEPTED` until all are true:
- existing GitHub workflows have been inventoried and reconciled;
- `python-quality-gate.yml` exists and is green on required triggers;
- Conda-based `python-compatibility.yml` exists and is green for every supported Python version;
- `nightly-classic-live.yml` exists and has a proven execution path on a valid Classic-capable runner;
- Sonar analysis is not duplicated;
- branch-protection status-check names are documented;
- workflow failure semantics, permissions, concurrency, timeouts and secret redaction are verified;
- all mandatory GitHub Actions release gates are green.

**Invariant:** GitHub Actions is the orchestrator. `tools/quality_gate.py`, canonical tests and canonical live runners remain the source of truth. Do not create a second CI/test implementation inside workflow YAML.
